Here is a complete, working example of how to extract all links from a website using **JSoup**.

This example uses the **Selenium** library (which is the standard way to use JSoup in Java) to automate the request and parse the HTML.

### Prerequisites
You need to have the **Selenium** library installed. You can install it via Maven or Gradle:
```bash
# Maven
mvn install -Dmaven.compiler.source=11 -Dmaven.compiler.target=11

# Gradle
gradle install -Dgradle.user.home=...
```

### Java Code

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;
import org.jsoup.safety.Safifier;
import org.jsoup.select.Elements;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.List;

public class LinkExtractor {

    // Define the URL to extract links from
    private static final String URL = "https://example.com";

    public static List<Element> extractLinks(String url) throws IOException {
        // 1. Create a new Document from the URL
        Document doc = Jsoup.parse(url);

        // 2. Safify the document to remove HTML tags (like <a> tags) and other noise
        // This makes the code cleaner and easier to read
        doc = Jsoup.safify(doc);

        // 3. Select all elements with class 'a' (links)
        // Note: You may need to adjust the class name based on your website
        List<Element> links = Elements.select("a");

        // 4. Print the count of links found
        System.out.println("Found " + links.size() + " links.");

        // 5. Print the first few links for debugging
        if (links.size() > 0) {
            System.out.println("First link: " + links.get(0).text());
            System.out.println("Second link: " + links.get(1).text());
        }

        return links;
    }

    // Example usage
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            List<Element> links = extractLinks(URL);
            System.out.println("Extracted " + links.size() + " links from " + URL);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

### How it works:
1.  **`Jsoup.parse(url)`**: Creates a new HTML document from the URL.
2.  **`Jsoup.safify(doc)`**: This is crucial. It removes HTML tags like `<a>`, `<img>`, and `<p>`. It also removes CSS selectors (like `.a`) that might be used to select elements.
3.  **`Elements.select("a")`**: This selects all elements that have the class `a`.
4.  **`System.out.println`**: Prints the text content of each selected element.

### Important Notes:
*   **Class Names**: The code above assumes the links have a class named `a`. If your website uses a different class name (e.g., `li` or `span`), you must change the selector to match.
*   **Selenium**: This code relies on Selenium to actually fetch the page and parse the HTML. If you are using a simple `HttpGet` request in Java without Selenium, you cannot easily get the HTML content.

