        var items = new List<object>
        {
            new Person { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 },
            new Person { Name = "Bob", Age = 25 },
            new Person { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 }, // Duplicate
            new Person { Name = "Charlie", Age = 35 },
            new Person { Name = "Bob", Age = 25 }, // Duplicate
            new Person { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 }, // Duplicate
            new Person { Name = "Diana", Age = 28 }
        };

        // Sort the collection based on the 'Age' property
        // The 'OrderBy' method sorts the collection in ascending order by the specified property.
        // 'Distinct' ensures that only unique values are returned.
        var sortedItems = items.OrderBy(item => item.Age).Distinct();

        // Display the unique results
        Console.WriteLine("Sorted by Age (Unique):");
        foreach (var item in sortedItems)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Name: {item.Name}, Age: {item.Age}");
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation

1.  **`items.OrderBy(item => item.Age).Distinct()`**:
    *   `items.OrderBy(item => item.Age)` creates a new collection that sorts the original list based on the `Age` property in ascending order.
    *   `.Distinct()` filters out any duplicate items. Since the original list contained duplicates (e.g., multiple `Person` objects with the same `Age`), this ensures that only the unique ages are returned.

2.  **`foreach` Loop**:
    *   The loop iterates through the resulting unique list, printing the `Name` and `Age` for each item.

3.  **`using` Statement**:
    *   The `using` statement is included to ensure that the `Items` variable is automatically cleaned up when the `Program` class is closed, preventing memory leaks.

