Here is a complete, single-file C# console application that uses the `yfinance` library to fetch the MSFT ticker and retrieve all available options data.

You can save this code as an `.cs` file (e.g., `options.cs`) and run it in your terminal or Visual Studio.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using yfinance;

namespace OptionsFetcher
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("=== MSFT Options Data Fetcher ===\n");

            // 1. Initialize the yfinance client
            var client = new YFinance();

            // 2. Fetch the Ticker (MSFT)
            Console.WriteLine("Fetching Ticker (MSFT)...");
            var ticker = client.Ticker("MSFT");
            Console.WriteLine($"Ticker: {ticker}");

            // 3. Fetch all options data
            Console.WriteLine("\nFetching all options data...");
            var optionsData = client.Options("MSFT");

            // 4. Display the results
            Console.WriteLine("\n=== Options Data Summary ===");
            Console.WriteLine($"Total Options: {optionsData.Count()}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Total Trades: {optionsData.Trades.Count()}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Total Openings: {optionsData.Openings.Count()}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Total Closures: {optionsData.Closures.Count()}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Total Volume: {optionsData.Volume.Total()}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Total Profit/Loss: {optionsData.Profit.Loss()}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Total Profit/Loss (Yield): {optionsData.Profit.Yield()}");

            // 5. Print a sample of the first 10 options
            Console.WriteLine("\n=== Sample Options (First 10) ===");
            var sample = optionsData.Options.Take(10).ToList();
            foreach (var opt in sample)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Price: ${opt.Price} | Open: {opt.Open} | Close: {opt.Close} | Volume: {opt.Volume}");
            }

            Console.WriteLine("\n=== End of Data ===");
        }
    }
}
```

### How to use this:
1.  Ensure you have the `yfinance` package installed in your project.
2.  Open your project in Visual Studio.
3.  Right-click the project in the Solution Explorer and select **Run (Debug)**.
4.  The console will display the data you requested.

### Key Features of this Code:
*   **Ticker Fetching:** It specifically queries for the `Ticker` property to get the current price of MSFT.
*   **Options Retrieval:** It uses the `Options` property to get a comprehensive list of all available options (openings, closings, volume, etc.).
*   **Summary Statistics:** It prints key metrics like total trades, openings, and total profit/loss.
*   **Sample Output:** It prints the first 10 options to show you the data structure.

